//
// A file asserts a REFUSAL and still runs every cell the refusal did not touch.
//
// Until loft#1242 a `@EXPECT_ERROR` ended the file: `run_test` stopped at "errors consumed"
// before execution, so an `assert` in any other function was compiled and never run and the file
// passed on the refusal alone. The corpus worked around it with a companion `<n>b-…` file — 32 of
// them — each existing only to hold the half that runs.
//
// The cells were always independent: the harness discovers every zero-parameter function as its
// own entry point and runs each under `catch_unwind`. Only the PARSE was shared. So the errors
// are attributed to their enclosing function, those cells are blanked, and the rest re-parsed and
// run — with the declarations verified once, against the real source.
//
// ⚠ THE CELLS BELOW MUST ACTUALLY RUN, and that is the whole claim, so each one asserts something
// that is FALSE if it does not execute. A guard for "this now runs" whose cells could silently not
// run would be the defect wearing the costume of its own fix. Verified by breaking one on purpose
// while writing this: `1 of 2 functions failed … assertion failed` — the isolated cells are
// scored, not merely executed.
//
// The refusing cell keeps an `assert` of its own. That is deliberate and is the shape R1 always
// allowed: an assertion inside the refused function is the USE that makes the refused expression
// matter, and it is unreachable by construction.
//
// @falsified-at: 9b9a74ab — MEASURED by hand, and the channel is the harness, not the program.
//   `make falsify` cannot score this one: it compares two BINARIES on one guard, and the change
//   is in `tests/wrap.rs`, which is the runner rather than the compiler — both binaries answer
//   identically here. On the parent, `wrap.rs` stops this file at its expected error and neither
//   `1242 RAN` line is printed; the file still passes, which is exactly the defect. With the fix
//   both lines print and a broken assertion in either cell fails the suite.
//
//   CHANNEL: the HARNESS, not the program. The change this guard catches is in
//   `tests/wrap.rs` — the runner, not the compiler — so both binaries answer identically and
//   falsify, which compares two BINARIES on one guard, structurally cannot score it.
//   HOLDS: every channel is equal between the two builds, which is why the sweep says INERT.
//   Re-validate through the corpus runner's own behaviour, by hand, as the receipt records.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot change type from hash
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.

struct E1242 { k: integer }

// The refusal: a `vector<text>` has no reading as records of a `hash<E1242[k]>` (`C-Only`).
// Its own assertion is unreachable, which is what "the cell is refused" means.
fn test_1242_an_unholdable_source_is_refused() {
  h1242: hash<E1242[k]> = [];
  bad1242: vector<text> = ["x"];
  h1242 += bad1242;
  assert(false, "unreached — this cell does not compile");
}

// …and these run anyway, which is the point of the file.
fn test_1242_a_sibling_cell_still_runs() {
  h1242b: hash<E1242[k]> = [];
  h1242b += [E1242 { k: 7 }];
  assert(len(h1242b) == 1, "a cell beside a refusal executes: {len(h1242b)}");
  got = h1242b[7];
  assert(got.k == 7, "and its assertions are really checked: {got.k}");
  println("1242 RAN");
}

fn test_1242_a_second_sibling_cell_runs_too() {
  v1242: vector<integer> = [];
  v1242 += [3];
  v1242 += [4];
  assert(len(v1242) == 2, "every surviving cell runs, not just the first: {len(v1242)}");
  println("1242 RAN TWICE");
}
